/* THE READER'S OWN BOUQUET
   ════════════════════════

   Not our drawing. This is the flower vase the owner of this app drew by hand
   and handed over, traced here from their original file — the same gerbera,
   daisy, tulip and cosmos, the same three butterflies, the same strawberry
   marks down the side of the pot.

   Two things were done to it and nothing else.

   THE GEOMETRY WAS SIMPLIFIED. The original carried eight hundred kilobytes of
   path data — every wobble of a drawing tablet recorded at full resolution —
   and it renders here about a hundred pixels wide, so the curves were refit to
   a tolerance far below what the screen can resolve. Rendered side by side at
   300px the two are indistinguishable.

   THE PALETTE WAS BOUND TO VARIABLES, one per colour she actually used, read
   off the file rather than guessed at. Nothing was merged and no hue was
   changed: --v-rose-3 is her rose. What the variables buy is night — a drawing
   frozen at full daylight saturation sits on a dark room like a sticker, so at
   night every colour drops in lightness and loses about a third of its chroma,
   which is what colour does in the dark. See nook.module.css.

   Generated. If the drawing changes, regenerate rather than hand-editing. */

